```
const standDown = useCallback(() => {
  setDecision((current) => {
    if (!current?.session) return current;
    current.session.release();   // side effect inside the updater
    return { subject: current.subject };
  });
}, []);
```
React state updaters must be pure: they are double-invoked in StrictMode and may be invoked for renders that are then discarded (concurrent rendering / Suspense). `release()` is idempotent per handle, so the double-invoke is survivable, but a discarded render leaves the Web Lock released while the state change never commits — the component would then still report `durable: true` and keep its `DurableYorkieProvider` mounted with the stable client key while holding no election, which is precisely the two-tabs-one-actor state the election exists to prevent. Releasing outside the updater (read the session from a ref, then `setDecision`) removes the hazard.
